From 979740c13c4a1582b0de4f3a4f39965a83802fd3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 02:15:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 06/41] tee: qseecom: reject a request too short for the
 address it must carry

The address-patch descriptor names an offset into the request to write a
buffer's physical address at, and the offset was checked with

	if (off > req_size - width)

which wraps when the request is shorter than the width. A caller passing a
zero-length request as parameter 0 therefore has every offset accepted, and the
offset lands in a put_unaligned_le32()/le64() against the staging buffer -- an
attacker-chosen offset for a kernel write, reachable by anyone who can open the
client device and start a session.

Check the length before subtracting from it.

While here, bound the staging allocation. Its size is the sum of the request,
the response and every patched buffer; all of those are individually bounded by
the shared memory they refer to, so a wrapped total should not be reachable, but
the total is what the buffer is sized from and the copies that follow trust it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
---
 drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index c3009aef5..9cbb9f201 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
@@ -65,6 +65,11 @@
 /* Physical alignment TZ requires of an application image. */
 #define QSEECOM_TEE_IMAGE_ALIGN		SZ_4M
 
+/* Sanity bound on one invoke's staging buffer, request plus response plus
+ * every patched buffer.
+ */
+#define QSEECOM_TEE_MAX_XFER		SZ_16M
+
 /* Sanity bound on an application image, so a bad size cannot exhaust memory. */
 #define QSEECOM_TEE_MAX_IMAGE		SZ_16M
 
@@ -652,7 +657,12 @@ static int qseecom_tee_check_patch(struct tee_param *voff,
 	if (width != 4 && width != 8)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	if (off > req_size - width)
+	/*
+	 * Both halves matter. Without the first, a request shorter than the
+	 * width makes the subtraction wrap and every offset passes, which puts
+	 * an attacker-chosen offset into a put_unaligned() further down.
+	 */
+	if (req_size < width || off > req_size - width)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	shm = pbuf->u.memref.shm;
@@ -735,6 +745,15 @@ static int qseecom_tee_invoke_func(struct tee_context *ctx,
 		for (k = 2; k < arg->num_params; k += 2)
 			need += param[k + 1].u.memref.size;
 
+		/*
+		 * Every size here is bounded by the shared memory it refers to,
+		 * so this should not be reachable -- but the total is what the
+		 * staging buffer is sized from, and a wrapped total would size
+		 * it far too small for the copies that follow.
+		 */
+		if (need < req_size || need > QSEECOM_TEE_MAX_XFER)
+			return -EINVAL;
+
 		bc.initial_size = PAGE_ALIGN(need) + PAGE_SIZE;
 		bc.max_size = bc.initial_size;
 
